        new("l-b2-8", "B2", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "Podcast: Why We Forget Names",
            "Welcome back to the Curious Mind podcast. Here's a situation you probably know well. You meet someone at a party, they tell you their name, and ten seconds later it's gone. Why does this happen? One reason is attention. When we're introduced, we're often busy thinking about what to say next, or worrying about the impression we're making. So the name never really gets stored in the first place. Another reason is that names are, in a sense, arbitrary. If I tell you someone is a baker, your brain connects that word to bread, smells and early mornings. But a name like Anvar or Helen doesn't connect to anything, so it's harder to hold on to. So what can you do? Try repeating the name out loud straight away, as in, nice to meet you, Anvar. Then link it to something vivid, perhaps a friend with the same name. And use it once more before you say goodbye. It feels awkward at first, but it really works.",
            [
