            "Podcast: Why Habits Stick",
            "Welcome back to The Curious Mind. Today we're exploring a question that has probably frustrated every single one of you at some point. Why is it so hard to build good habits, and so annoyingly easy to keep bad ones? Researchers who study behaviour describe a habit as a kind of loop. There's a cue, something in your environment that triggers the behaviour. There's the routine itself. And there's a reward, which is what persuades your brain that the whole thing is worth repeating. The crucial point is that once a habit is established, it runs largely on autopilot. That's wonderful when the habit is brushing your teeth, and rather less wonderful when it's reaching for your phone the moment you feel bored. Contrary to the popular claim that it takes exactly twenty one days to form a habit, the research suggests the timeframe varies enormously, from a few weeks to the better part of a year, depending on the person and the behaviour. Later in the episode, I'll be speaking to a psychologist who argues that willpower is overrated, and that the smartest strategy is to redesign your surroundings so that the good choice becomes the easy one. Stay with us.",
            [
                new("What is the episode mainly about?", ["The history of the toothbrush", "Why willpower is the key to success", "How to use your phone less at work", "How habits work and why they are hard to change"], 3, "The host asks, \"Why is it so hard to build good habits, and so annoyingly easy to keep bad ones?\""),
                new("According to the host, what is the role of the reward?", ["It triggers the behaviour in the first place", "It replaces the routine", "It persuades the brain that the behaviour is worth repeating", "It makes the habit disappear"], 2, "The host says the reward \"is what persuades your brain that the whole thing is worth repeating\"."),
                new("What does research say about how long it takes to form a habit?", ["It varies greatly depending on the person and the behaviour", "It always takes exactly twenty-one days", "It takes at least two years", "It takes only a few days"], 0, "The host says \"the timeframe varies enormously\", \"depending on the person and the behaviour\"."),
                new("What does the guest psychologist argue?", ["Willpower is the most important factor", "It is smarter to redesign your surroundings than to rely on willpower", "Bad habits can never really be changed", "Rewards should be avoided completely"], 1, "The host says the psychologist \"argues that willpower is overrated\" and recommends redesigning \"your surroundings\"."),
            ])),
        new("l-c1-2", "C1", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "Lecture: Why Great Cities Grew Beside Rivers",
            "Good afternoon. In today's lecture, I want to consider a pattern so familiar that we rarely question it. If you look at a map of the world's great historic cities, a remarkable number of them sit on the banks of a river. Why should that be? The obvious answer is drinking water, but that's only the beginning. Rivers deposit fertile soil when they flood, which allowed early farmers to produce a surplus of food. And a surplus, crucially, is what frees some people from farming altogether, so they can become craftsmen, traders and officials. In other words, city dwellers. Rivers were also the motorways of the ancient world. Moving heavy goods by boat was vastly cheaper and faster than hauling them overland, so places where routes met, or where a river could easily be crossed, tended to flourish as markets. Here in Central Asia, the oasis cities along the Zarafshan depended on careful irrigation for their very existence. That said, rivers were a mixed blessing. The same floods that enriched the soil could sweep away homes, and standing water brought disease. So next week, we'll look at how early engineers tried to control rivers, with varying degrees of success.",
            [
                new("What is the lecture mainly about?", ["How modern motorways are planned", "Why so many historic cities developed beside rivers", "The causes of floods in Central Asia", "Why people are leaving farms today"], 1, "The lecturer notes that many great cities \"sit on the banks of a river\" and asks, \"Why should that be?\""),
                new("Why, according to the lecturer, was a food surplus important?", ["It made rivers flood more often", "It was sold to other continents", "It made boats cheaper to build", "It allowed some people to do jobs other than farming"], 3, "The lecturer says a surplus \"is what frees some people from farming altogether\"."),
                new("What does the lecturer compare rivers to?", ["The motorways of the ancient world", "The walls of a city", "Modern markets", "A map of the world"], 0, "The lecturer says, \"Rivers were also the motorways of the ancient world.\""),
                new("What will next week's lecture be about?", ["The history of boat building", "Diseases in modern cities", "How early engineers tried to control rivers", "Farming in the desert"], 2, "The lecturer says, \"Next week, we'll look at how early engineers tried to control rivers.\""),
            ])),
        new("l-c1-3", "C1", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "Radio Essay: In Praise of Boredom",
            "When did you last do absolutely nothing? I don't mean scrolling through your phone while waiting for a bus. I mean genuinely sitting with no entertainment at all. For many of us, the honest answer is that we can't remember. We've become remarkably efficient at eliminating boredom, and I want to suggest tonight that this may come at a hidden cost. Boredom, after all, is uncomfortable for a reason. It's a signal that our current situation isn't satisfying, a nudge to go and find something more meaningful to do. When we instantly silence that signal with a screen, we never discover where it might have led. There's also intriguing evidence linking boredom to creativity. In one study, participants who first completed a deliberately tedious task, copying numbers out of a phone book, later came up with more imaginative ideas than those who hadn't. The theory is that a bored mind starts to wander, and a wandering mind is where unexpected connections are made. I'm not suggesting we should seek out misery. But perhaps, the next time you're standing in a queue, you might resist the urge to reach for your pocket, and simply see where your thoughts take you.",
            [
                new("What is the speaker's main message?", ["Phones should be banned in public places", "Boredom is usually a sign of illness", "Boredom can be useful, so we should not always avoid it", "Queues in modern cities are getting longer"], 2, "The speaker suggests that \"eliminating boredom\" \"may come at a hidden cost\"."),
                new("According to the speaker, what is boredom a signal of?", ["That our current situation is not satisfying", "That we need more sleep", "That we are hungry", "That we are working too hard"], 0, "The speaker says, \"It's a signal that our current situation isn't satisfying.\""),
                new("What task did participants in the study do first?", ["They read a long novel", "They played a video game", "They sat in silence for an hour", "They copied numbers out of a phone book"], 3, "The speaker describes \"a deliberately tedious task, copying numbers out of a phone book\"."),
                new("What does the speaker suggest doing in a queue?", ["Reading the news", "Resisting the phone and letting your thoughts wander", "Calling a friend", "Planning the rest of your day"], 1, "The speaker suggests you \"resist the urge to reach for your pocket, and simply see where your thoughts take you\"."),
            ])),
        new("l-c1-4", "C1", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "Radio Interview: The Chef Who Wastes Nothing",
            "Good evening, and welcome to Kitchen Table. My guest tonight runs a small restaurant with an unusual promise. Nothing that comes into her kitchen goes into the bin. Not the carrot tops, not the stale bread, not even the coffee grounds. Now, you might assume this is simply a clever marketing idea, but when I visited her last month, I was genuinely struck by how seriously she takes it. Vegetable peelings become stock. Yesterday's bread is turned into a crisp crumb for salads or a rather wonderful dessert. Fruit that's past its best ends up in jams and drinks, and whatever truly can't be eaten is composted for the farm that supplies her vegetables. She tells me the approach began out of necessity rather than principle. When she opened, money was so tight that she simply couldn't afford to throw anything away. Only later did she realise how much food restaurants typically waste, and how much flavour was hiding in the parts most cooks discard. Tonight she'll share some of her techniques, and she's also going to challenge us, the home cooks, to rethink what we consider rubbish. So if you've got a notebook handy, I'd suggest keeping it close.",
            [
                new("What is the presenter mainly doing?", ["Introducing a chef whose restaurant avoids wasting food", "Reviewing an expensive restaurant", "Explaining how to grow vegetables on a farm", "Warning listeners about unhealthy coffee"], 0, "The presenter says the guest's promise is that \"nothing that comes into her kitchen goes into the bin\"."),
                new("What happens to yesterday's bread?", ["It is thrown away", "It is given to the farm", "It becomes a crumb for salads or a dessert", "It is used to make stock"], 2, "The presenter says, \"Yesterday's bread is turned into a crisp crumb for salads or a rather wonderful dessert.\""),
                new("Why did the chef first start avoiding waste?", ["She wanted good publicity", "She could not afford to throw anything away", "A customer asked her to", "A cooking school taught her to"], 1, "The presenter says \"money was so tight that she simply couldn't afford to throw anything away\"."),
                new("What will the chef challenge listeners to do?", ["Open their own restaurants", "Stop eating bread", "Visit her farm", "Rethink what they consider rubbish"], 3, "The presenter says she will \"challenge us, the home cooks, to rethink what we consider rubbish\"."),
            ])),
        new("l-c1-5", "C1", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "Talk: Losing the Night Sky",
            "Thank you all for coming. I'd like to begin with a simple question. When did you last see the Milky Way? If you live in a city, the answer may well be never. Astronomers estimate that a large share of the world's population now lives under skies so brightened by artificial light that our own galaxy is effectively invisible. This is what we call light pollution, and unlike many environmental problems, it's remarkably easy to overlook, precisely because it's the absence of something. Yet its effects reach far beyond disappointed stargazers. Migrating birds, which navigate partly by the stars, can become disoriented by brightly lit buildings. Newly hatched sea turtles, which instinctively head towards the brightest horizon, may crawl inland towards streetlights instead of towards the sea. And in humans, bright light late at night can disrupt the body clock and interfere with sleep. The encouraging part is that, unlike most forms of pollution, this one can be reversed almost instantly. Shielding streetlights so they point downwards, choosing warmer colours and simply switching off lights that nobody needs can make a dramatic difference. Several towns have already been recognised for protecting their dark skies, and visitors now travel specifically to see them.",
            [
                new("What is the talk mainly about?", ["How to become an astronomer", "Why cities need more streetlights", "The migration routes of sea turtles", "The effects of light pollution and how to reduce it"], 3, "The speaker introduces \"what we call light pollution\", describes its effects and then its solutions."),
                new("Why, according to the speaker, is light pollution easy to overlook?", ["Because it only happens in summer", "Because it is the absence of something", "Because it only affects birds", "Because it is very rare"], 1, "The speaker says it is easy to overlook \"precisely because it's the absence of something\"."),
                new("What problem can newly hatched sea turtles face?", ["They cannot see the moon", "They hatch too early in the year", "They may crawl towards streetlights instead of the sea", "They are attacked by migrating birds"], 2, "The speaker says they \"may crawl inland towards streetlights instead of towards the sea\"."),
                new("Which solution does the speaker mention?", ["Shielding streetlights so they point downwards", "Building taller buildings", "Using brighter white lights", "Keeping all lights on through the night"], 0, "The speaker mentions \"shielding streetlights so they point downwards\" as a solution."),
            ])),
        new("l-c1-6", "C1", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "Lecture: The Hidden Life of Soil",
            "Good morning. Today I'd like you to look down rather than up, because the ground beneath your feet is one of the most crowded places on the planet. A single handful of healthy soil contains more living organisms than there are people on Earth. Most of them are bacteria and fungi, far too small to see. For a long time, we treated soil as little more than a place to anchor plants and store water. That view has changed dramatically. We now know that many fungi form partnerships with plant roots. The fungus spreads fine threads through the soil, reaching water and minerals the roots can't access, and in return the plant supplies it with sugars. It's a trade that has existed for hundreds of millions of years. The practical consequences are significant. Heavy ploughing and certain chemicals can break these networks apart, which may leave crops more dependent on fertiliser. So some farmers are now experimenting with disturbing the soil as little as possible. The results so far are promising, though, as ever, more long-term research is needed before we draw firm conclusions.",
            [
                new("What is the main focus of the lecture?", ["The complex living systems found in soil", "How to choose the right fertiliser", "The history of ploughing", "Why plants need sunlight"], 0, "The lecturer says \"the ground beneath your feet is one of the most crowded places on the planet\" and describes the organisms in it."),
                new("How was soil viewed for a long time?", ["As the most important source of food", "As something that should be ploughed daily", "As a dangerous source of disease", "Mainly as a place to anchor plants and store water"], 3, "The lecturer says, \"We treated soil as little more than a place to anchor plants and store water.\""),
                new("What do plants give fungi in return?", ["Water", "Minerals", "Sugars", "Shelter from bacteria"], 2, "The lecturer says, \"In return the plant supplies it with sugars.\""),
                new("How does the lecturer describe the results of disturbing soil less?", ["Completely proven", "Promising, but in need of more long-term research", "Disappointing so far", "Too expensive to measure"], 1, "The lecturer says, \"The results so far are promising, though, as ever, more long-term research is needed.\""),
            ])),
        new("l-c1-7", "C1", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "Radio Essay: Why We Keep Old Letters",
            "In the drawer of my desk there's a shoebox full of letters. Some are from my grandmother, written in careful, slanting handwriting. Others are from a school friend who moved abroad when we were twelve. I rarely read them, yet I've carried that box through four house moves. Why? I suspect it's because a letter is not simply information. An email tells you what someone said. A letter also tells you how they were when they said it. You can see where the pen pressed harder, where a word was crossed out, where a coffee cup left a faint ring. The object itself becomes a kind of witness. There's also the question of permanence. Our messages today are abundant but oddly fragile. They sit on servers we don't control, and a forgotten password can erase years of conversation. Paper, by contrast, simply waits. I'm not suggesting we abandon our phones and return to stamps. But perhaps, once or twice a year, it's worth writing to someone by hand. You're not just sending words. You're sending something they might keep for the rest of their lives.",
            [
                new("What is the central idea of the essay?", ["People should stop using phones and emails", "Old letters should be stored in museums", "Handwriting is becoming harder to read", "Handwritten letters carry a personal and lasting value that digital messages often lack"], 3, "The speaker says a letter is \"not simply information\" and that the reader might keep it \"for the rest of their lives\"."),
                new("What does the speaker say about how often they read the letters?", ["Every evening", "Only after moving house", "Rarely", "Once a year on a special date"], 2, "The speaker says, \"I rarely read them, yet I've carried that box through four house moves.\""),
                new("According to the speaker, what does a letter show that an email does not?", ["How the writer was when they wrote it", "The exact date it was written", "The writer's true opinions", "Who else read it"], 0, "The speaker says, \"A letter also tells you how they were when they said it.\""),
                new("Why does the speaker call digital messages fragile?", ["They are often written too quickly", "They can be lost, for example through a forgotten password", "They are easy to misunderstand", "They are read by too many people"], 1, "The speaker says, \"A forgotten password can erase years of conversation.\""),
            ])),
        new("l-c1-8", "C1", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "Talk: Streets Designed for People",
            "Thank you for having me. I'd like to begin with a simple question. Who is your street really for? In many cities over the last century, the honest answer has been cars. Roads were widened, pavements narrowed, and crossings were placed wherever they least disrupted traffic. The result is that walking, which is the most natural form of transport we have, often became unpleasant and even risky. Increasingly, though, planners are questioning that approach. Some of the most interesting changes are surprisingly modest. Widening a pavement by a metre, planting a row of trees or adding benches can transform how a street feels. People linger, local shops see more customers, and children can walk to school on their own. Of course, these changes are rarely uncontroversial. Business owners often fear that removing parking will drive customers away. Yet in many cases, studies have found the opposite, because people on foot tend to visit more often, even if they spend less each time. My argument isn't that cars should disappear. It's that a street should be designed around the people who use it, not merely those passing through it.",
            [
                new("What is the speaker's main argument?", ["Cars should be banned from city centres", "Streets should be designed around the people who use them", "Shops should move out of city centres", "Cities need more parking spaces"], 1, "The speaker says, \"A street should be designed around the people who use it, not merely those passing through it.\""),
                new("According to the speaker, who have streets mostly been designed for?", ["Children", "Shop owners", "Cyclists", "Cars"], 3, "The speaker says, \"In many cities over the last century, the honest answer has been cars.\""),
                new("What do business owners often fear?", ["That removing parking will drive customers away", "That trees will block their windows", "That benches will attract noise", "That rents will go up"], 0, "The speaker says, \"Business owners often fear that removing parking will drive customers away.\""),
                new("What have studies found about people on foot?", ["They spend more each time", "They rarely go into shops", "They tend to visit shops more often", "They prefer large shopping centres"], 2, "The speaker says \"people on foot tend to visit more often, even if they spend less each time\"."),
            ])),
        new("l-c1-9", "C1", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "Podcast: The Psychology of Queuing",
            "Welcome to Everyday Science. Today's topic is something we all do and almost nobody enjoys. Waiting in line. What's fascinating is that researchers have found that how long we wait often matters less than how the wait feels. Take unoccupied time. Minutes spent standing with nothing to do seem much longer than minutes spent doing something. That's partly why some buildings put mirrors next to lifts. People look at themselves, and complaints about slow lifts tend to fall. Uncertainty makes things worse, too. A wait of unknown length feels longer than a known one, which is why a simple sign saying, about ten minutes from here, can calm a crowd. But perhaps the most powerful factor is fairness. People will tolerate a surprisingly long wait if they believe everyone is being treated equally. What makes them furious is seeing someone push in, or noticing that the other line is moving faster. In fact, many banks and airports now use a single long line that feeds several desks. It isn't necessarily quicker, but it feels fairer, and that turns out to be what we care about most.",
