                new("What is the main idea of the episode?", ["Queues in banks are getting shorter", "Lifts should be made faster", "How a wait feels matters more than how long it actually is", "Airports need more staff"], 2, "The host says \"how long we wait often matters less than how the wait feels\"."),
                new("Why do some buildings put mirrors next to lifts?", ["People look at themselves and complain less about slow lifts", "To make the space look bigger", "To help security staff watch people", "To make the lifts actually move faster"], 0, "The host says, \"People look at themselves, and complaints about slow lifts tend to fall.\""),
                new("According to the host, what is perhaps the most powerful factor?", ["Comfort", "Uncertainty", "Boredom", "Fairness"], 3, "The host says, \"Perhaps the most powerful factor is fairness.\""),
                new("Why do banks and airports use a single line feeding several desks?", ["It is always much faster", "It feels fairer, even if it isn't necessarily quicker", "It needs fewer staff", "It takes up less space"], 1, "The host says, \"It isn't necessarily quicker, but it feels fairer.\""),
            ])),
        new("l-c1-10", "C1", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "Interview: A Translator on Words That Don't Travel",
            "People assume translation is about finding the matching word in another language. In my experience, that's the least of it. The real work is carrying over a feeling, a tone, sometimes even a joke, and those almost never have neat equivalents. I once spent an entire afternoon on a single greeting. In the original, an elderly character welcomes a guest with a long, warm formula that goes on for several lines. In English, that sounds absurdly formal, almost comic. But if I shortened it to a simple hello, the reader would lose any sense of this man's generosity. In the end I kept part of it and let the rest show through his actions in the next paragraph. That's typical. Readers never see the version I rejected, of course, but those invisible decisions are what the job really consists of. You're constantly making trade-offs, and you have to accept that something will be lost. What I find encouraging, though, is that something can also be gained. A good translation sometimes reveals meanings that readers of the original took for granted. Being forced to explain a culture to outsiders makes you see it more clearly yourself.",
            [
                new("What is the translator's main point?", ["Translation is mainly about carrying over feeling and tone, not matching words", "Some languages are impossible to translate", "Machines will soon replace human translators", "English is too formal for literature"], 0, "The translator says, \"The real work is carrying over a feeling, a tone, sometimes even a joke.\""),
                new("Why was the greeting difficult to translate?", ["It contained words that don't exist in English", "It was a joke the translator didn't understand", "In English it would sound absurdly formal", "The author asked for it to be removed"], 2, "The translator says, \"In English, that sounds absurdly formal, almost comic.\""),
                new("How did the translator finally solve the problem?", ["Translated every word exactly", "Kept part of it and showed the rest through the character's actions", "Replaced it with a simple hello", "Added a note at the bottom of the page"], 1, "The translator says, \"I kept part of it and let the rest show through his actions in the next paragraph.\""),
                new("What does the translator find encouraging?", ["Readers rarely notice what is lost", "Translators are now better paid", "Most jokes translate easily", "Translation can reveal meanings that were taken for granted"], 3, "The translator says, \"A good translation sometimes reveals meanings that readers of the original took for granted.\""),
            ])),
        new("l-c1-11", "C1", new ComprehensionExercise(
            "Lecture: Ice as an Archive of the Past",
            "Good afternoon. Today we're looking at one of the most remarkable records of our planet's history, and it's made of ice. In places like Antarctica and Greenland, snow has fallen for an immensely long time without ever melting. Each year's snow is buried under the next, and gradually it's compressed into solid ice. Crucially, as this happens, tiny bubbles of air become trapped inside. Those bubbles are, in effect, samples of the atmosphere as it was when the snow fell. By drilling deep into the ice and extracting long cylinders, known as cores, scientists can study conditions stretching back hundreds of thousands of years. The layers can reveal far more than the composition of the air. Ash may point to a distant volcanic eruption. Dust suggests periods when the climate was drier and windier. Even the chemistry of the ice itself provides clues about past temperatures. Of course, interpreting these signals is complex, and different cores must be compared carefully. But taken together, they give us an extraordinarily detailed picture of how the climate has changed, and that helps us understand how it might change in the future.",
            [
